From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 18:47:51 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] http: align supported content encodings with Chrome

Fresh local Chrome 154 fingerprint captures send gzip, deflate, br, zstd
on navigation and Accept-CH fetch follow-ups; Lightpanda sent deflate,
gzip, br. Enable actual zstd decoding rather than advertising a token
which curl cannot handle.

Pin zstd 1.5.7 by its Zig content hash. Build only its modern decoder and
common sources as a target-aware static library, with portable C decoding
and no legacy formats or compressor. Export its public headers and enable
curl's existing native streaming zstd decoder through HAVE_ZSTD.

Order browser-default Accept-Encoding as gzip, deflate, br, zstd for
potentially trustworthy URLs, and gzip, deflate for ordinary insecure
HTTP, matching the measured Chrome policy (including trusted loopback).
Apply this to navigation, fetch, XHR and other non-internal subresources;
recompute on redirects and preserve explicit headers, including empty
values. Keep CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING decoding enabled and leave internal
requests on curl's defaults.

Validation: release build and 1658 existing tests passed. Ignored local
fixtures passed 228 assertions across both browsers for HTTPS HTTP/2,
HTTPS HTTP/1.1, loopback/private HTTP, supported codec decoding, chunked
zstd, malformed zstd, XHR/scripts/styles, redirects and explicit overrides.
The unmodified fingerprint page matched this header in three fresh
processes per browser for both navigation and follow-up. The full TLS
gate retained all eight passes with 20 samples per side per scenario.
No new committed tests. Linux Chrome remains the practical transport
reference, not proof of macOS identity or complete HTTP stack parity.
